  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Valley rain and mountain snow Sunday into Monday morning,\n   focused along and south of I-90. Northwest Montana stays mostly\n   dry.  \n\n-  High pressure bringing stable conditions and valley fog next\n   week. \n\n-  Late Week Shift: A return to unsettled pattern and potential\n   for mountain snow by next weekend. \n\nLow pressure continues to spin in place over the Pacific Northwest\nthis morning. This feature brought sporadic showers to the region\nyesterday and due to the breaks in clouds, some locations have\ndeveloped fog or low stratus clouds. For Halloween festivities\nlater today, expect dry conditions and partly cloudy skies.\n\nLooking further upstream over the northeastern Pacific Ocean, a\nvery large trough has been shifting eastwards over the last 24\nhours. As a result, our Pacific Northwest trough will begin to\nmove and split into two separate systems. This second system will\ndraw up subtropical moisture over the western United States,\nwith our area receiving the northern edge of it, generally south\nof I-90 during the day on Sunday, and pulling away Sunday night.\nThere is a high probability for at least a tenth of an inch of\nprecipitation east and south of a line from McCall, Idaho to Sula,\nMontana to Great Falls. The mountains could see 0.25 to 0.50\" of\nprecipitation. Northwest Montana may avoid most of the\nprecipitation Sunday, and sunshine could actually peek out between\nhigh clouds! \n\nSnow levels will be around 7000 feet to start but lower to 5000\nfeet late Sunday. This will bring a chance for snow at mountain\npasses and locations including Lost Trail, Georgetown Lake area,\nHomestake Pass, and passes along the Divide in Lemhi County (i.e.\nLemhi Pass), where lows by Monday morning will be at or below\nfreezing. Impacts will primarily be slushy accumulations at these\nlocations. \n\nHigh pressure builds by Monday morning, but fog and low clouds\ncould again be impactful, especially areas that received rain the\nday before: West-central and southwest Montana, parts of central \nIdaho, and Lemhi County. Clouds give away to sunshine on Monday\ndue to a transient ridge. \n\nChances for fog or low clouds continue Tuesday and Wednesday \nmornings, while daytime temperatures climb to slightly above-\naverage readings. The pattern becomes progressively more unsettled\nby the end of the week. Late week mountain snowfall will depend on\nhow much energy can break off from the huge trough over the\nnortheast Pacific and move onshore, similar to what we have been\nexperiencing lately. The latest NBM forecast snow levels next\nweekend range from 3000 to 5500 feet.",
